# sha256 of each prebuilt libmonero_wallet2_api_c, by platform directory.
#
# This file is the trust anchor for ./fetch-libs.sh. The libraries themselves
# are gitignored and distributed as release assets, which are mutable — anyone
# with write access can replace an asset at the same URL and nothing in git
# changes. These hashes are in git, so a swap shows up as a diff on a commit
# instead of as a silently different binary linked into a wallet.
#
# The hash is of the UNCOMPRESSED library, the artifact that actually gets
# linked. That keeps it stable if the assets are ever recompressed, and the
# .tar.xz carries its own integrity check for transport.
#
# Written by ./publish-libs.sh. Verify a rebuild against it with:
#     cd lib && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS      # shasum -a 256 -c on macOS
#
# PLACEHOLDER — NOTHING IS PUBLISHED YET.
#
# The hashes below are real: all thirteen files came from one run of the
# Dockerfile in this directory, at the monero_c pin below, on 2026-09-17. The
# Android 16 KB LOAD-alignment check passed on all five files it covers and the
# macOS dylibs carry @executable_path, so each library was checked for the shape
# the wallet actually needs, not merely produced.
#
# The Android platforms now carry both of their files. libc++_shared.so ships
# beside the wallet library because monero_c links libc++ dynamically and
# wallet/android/bind.sh wants the NDK copy the library was built with. It is an
# NDK r28c prebuilt rather than something this build compiles, which is why the
# same bytes turn up in any tree using that NDK pin.
#
# THESE ARE REPRODUCIBLE, WHICH THE PREVIOUS PLACEHOLDER'S WERE NOT. Rebuilding
# a target from an unchanged tree recompiles and relinks to byte-identical
# output. That required pinning GIT_COMMITTER_DATE in the Dockerfile: before it,
# apply_patches.sh committed monero_c's patches into the monero submodule with
# the current time, the commit hash moved, and monero compiled that hash into
# its version string — so every build differed. The hashes this file used to
# carry came from such a build and can never be reproduced.
#
# It reproduces only through the Dockerfile. Build paths are compiled into these
# libraries and the Dockerfile fixes them at /monero_c, while build-darwin.sh and
# build-ios.sh build under .monero_c-darwin / .monero_c-ios; a Mac-built Apple
# library will not match these. The thirteen files here all came from one Docker
# run, so they are mutually consistent. See README.md.
#
# Still unchecked: one target of the ten has been built twice, that rebuild
# reused the cached toolchain and dependencies, and nobody has reproduced these
# bytes on a second machine. (An earlier note here claimed a double build proved
# determinism. It did not: that pair reused one prepared checkout, so the patch
# commit — the thing that actually varied — was never re-made between them.)
#
# What is missing is the release. No assets have been uploaded, so this file
# is not yet an anchor for anything a third party can fetch. Note the absence
# of a "# Release:" line: fetch-libs.sh reads the tag from that line, so
# without it the script stops with an explicit message rather than downloading
# from a release that has no assets. Do not add one by hand — ./publish-libs.sh
# writes it, together with the hashes of exactly what it uploaded, so the tag
# and the hashes always describe the same release.
#
# So this commit says "these are the bytes this tree produces", not "these are
# the bytes you will be served". Treat the commit where publish-libs.sh rewrites
# this file as the point where the anchor starts meaning something.
#
